WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000 position:98%
The majority of traditional programming environments only work in digital, in machine-like precision.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.000 position:97%
Mathematica, though, its primary function gives you the ability to do analytical calculations.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000 position:98%
That’s already a big plus that you don’t find in any other traditional environment.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:25.000 position:97%
I discovered Mathematica in the very first versions, in the 1980s and 90s.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:35.000 position:97%
It was a veritable revolution to discover the first formal calculation tools offered by Mathematica,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:43.000 position:97%
which... allowed me to... apply mathematics on a computer almost as I think of it.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:53.000 position:97%
You are able to replace things in very few lines of code, things that, in normal environments, would take dozens, hundreds of lines of code,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.000 position:91%
that you replace in Mathematica with one or two functions.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.000 position:75%
Well, I have an undergrad degree in engineering,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000 position:83%
and then I did a doctorate in mechanical engineering.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.000 position:98%
Currently I am an instructor-researcher, an associate professor at the École nationale d’ingénieurs (National Engineering Institute) in Metz.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.000 position:92%
I conduct my research in a mechanics lab, and I teach math,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:29.000 position:97%
notably in the classroom, but just as much in my research and in the supervision of industrial projects with students.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:39.000 position:98%
I decided to present two fairly important examples of the value of Mathematica in teaching:

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:49.000 position:97%
the first example shows the solution of the equation of vibrating cords using the classic technique of the Fourier series.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:58.000 position:97%
The second example is... the approximate solution of partial derivative equations.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.000 position:97%
with Mathematica, we can really get back to the base of these methods,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.000 position:63%
which are the analytical approximations.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:12.000 position:98%
This way, they understand the mathematical concept; they manage to implement it very easily in these simple examples.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:21.000 position:98%
Our lecture halls are systematically equipped with video projectors and graphic tablets

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000 position:75%
linked to a computer that has Mathematica on it.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:36.000 position:98%
When I’m writing my test, I allow the students to come to the test with their computer, with Mathematica installed on it.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:43.000 position:98%
Because I don’t see the point of... making them do lines and lines of calculations by hand;

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.000 position:58%
for me, that no longer has any value.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:54.000 position:97%
I had the opportunity... to test a variety of software for formal calculation,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:01.000 position:97%
and I was totally convinced of Mathematica’s superiority for a bunch of reasons,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:11.000 position:98%
for the... it’s sturdiness, the coherence of the set, and then, above all, the ease of programming.

